course: medicine
lesson: 12
title: COVID and staying home
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: COVID-19 is caused by a coronavirus called SARS-CoV-2; in most children it is mild, like a cold, with fever, cough, a sore throat or tiredness.
explain: In 2020 it spread across the whole world because nobody's body knew it yet: that is what a pandemic means.
explain: People wore masks, kept apart and stayed home to slow it down until vaccines arrived, built on decades of earlier research and tested on tens of thousands of people.
explain: Older people and those with other illnesses are more likely to get seriously ill, which is why we wash and gel our hands even when we feel fine.
explain: A cold, flu and COVID can look alike; only a test can tell them apart.
fact: COVID-19 is caused by the SARS-CoV-2 coronavirus, first found in 2019, which is where the 19 comes from, and in most children it is mild.
fact: UK advice now is that children with mild symptoms can go to school if they have no fever and feel well enough; people who test positive are advised to stay home for a few days.
fact: Older people and people with other health conditions are more likely to get seriously ill with COVID, partly because the body's defences work less well in old age; if a relative gets ill, it is not a child's fault.
fact: Masks catch some of the droplets that carry viruses and do not stop you getting enough oxygen.
fact: In 2020 and 2021 governments asked people to wear masks, keep apart and stay home to slow the spread before there were vaccines.
fact: COVID can damage the smell-sensing cells in the nose; smell usually returns within weeks, but for some it takes months; losing smell does not mean you are seriously ill.
fact: A lateral flow test looks for virus proteins in a nose or throat sample; a positive result is usually right, but a negative one can miss an early infection; the swab only goes a little way in and cannot touch the brain.
fact: A pandemic is a new disease spreading across many countries because nobody has immunity yet.
fact: COVID vaccines were made within a year using decades of earlier research and tested on tens of thousands of people.
fact: Some people have tiredness, breathlessness or brain fog for months after COVID, called long COVID; doctors are still studying why.
fact: Hand gel with alcohol kills many germs, but not all, such as norovirus; soap and water is better after the toilet or when hands are dirty.
exercise: What causes COVID-19? | A coronavirus called SARS-CoV-2 | A bacterium that lives in dirty water | Very cold weather in winter | Too much screen time indoors
exercise: How ill does COVID make most children? | Mildly, a bit like a cold | Always very ill, so they need hospital | Not at all, children can't catch it | Ill for a whole year without a break
exercise: Why are older people more at risk from COVID? | Their immune systems work less well | They don't wash their hands as often | They sleep a lot more in the day | They eat different kinds of food
exercise: Do masks stop you getting enough oxygen? | No, air gets through fine | Yes, after you wear one for an hour | Yes, but for children only | No, masks actually give you extra oxygen
exercise: Why did people stay home and keep apart in 2020? | To slow the spread before there were vaccines | Because all the shops ran out of food | Because it rained every single day that spring and summer | To save money on bus fares
exercise: Why can people lose their sense of smell with COVID? | The virus can damage smell cells in the nose | Their nose gets blocked up by wearing masks all day | Their brain slowly forgets smells | They stop eating
exercise: How does a lateral flow test work? | It looks for virus proteins in a nose or throat sample | It reads your brain waves through the inside of your nose | It measures your temperature | It counts how fast your heart beats
exercise: What is a pandemic? | A new disease spreading across many countries | An illness that stays inside one house | A kind of big frying pan | A very bad cold in just one town
exercise: How fast were the COVID vaccines made? | Within a year, using decades of earlier research | In one single week, with no testing at all | Slowly, over about fifty years | They were never made
exercise: What is long COVID? | Symptoms like tiredness lasting months after COVID | A COVID test that takes a long time to show its result line | A longer name for flu | Being off school for an extra day
exercise: You have a cold. How can you tell if it's COVID? | Only a test can tell | By the colour of your snot | By how loud and how often you sneeze | You can always tell just by looking
exercise: Does hand gel kill every germ? | No, some like norovirus survive it | Yes, every single germ there is | Yes, but only on Mondays | No, hand gel kills no germs at all, it just smells
exercise: When is soap and water better than gel? | When hands are dirty or after the toilet | Never, gel is always better | Only in summer when it is hot | Only for grown-ups, never for children at school
exercise: Is a negative lateral flow test always right? | No, it can miss an early infection | Yes, always, it never makes a mistake | Yes, if you do it at night before bed | No, negatives are never right, only positives
exercise: What does the 19 in COVID-19 stand for? | 2019, the year it was first found | The 19th virus ever found by scientists | The 19 days that the illness lasts | The 19 countries it reached first
test: tested positive but feel fine, can I go to Grandad's? | Better not; you could pass it to him and older people get sicker | Yeah, feeling fine means you can't spread it to anybody, even old people | Yeah, grandads can't catch it | No, you can never see him again
test: My cousin says the swab touches your brain. True? | No, swab tests don't touch the brain | Yes, it goes right up into it | Yes, but only a tiny bit at the very front | No, it touches the back of your eyes
test: Grandad went to hospital with COVID. Is it my fault because I had a cold? | No, it is not your fault if a relative gets ill | Yes, always, colds and COVID are the same | Yes, colds turn into COVID | No, but it's his own fault for getting old
test: Josh says the COVID vaccines were rushed with no tests. What's true? | They went through normal trials faster thanks to huge resources | He's right, there was no testing at all, they just sent them out to people | They were made in a day | They are just water
test: Uncle Mike is still tired months after COVID and someone called him lazy. Fair? | No, long COVID is real | Yes, he's just being lazy | Yes, it's all in his head | No, he's just bored at home
test: What would happen if a brand-new virus appeared and nobody had immunity? | It could spread across the world as a pandemic | Nothing, bodies already know all viruses | It would stay in one house | It would vanish at once by itself
test: In a film, a girl sniffs once and says "it's COVID, not a cold". What's wrong? | Only a test can tell them apart | Colds don't ever make you sniff | COVID makes you sneeze purple | Nothing, one sniff shows it clearly enough
test: Lost my smell when I had COVID, does that mean I'm seriously ill? | No, it doesn't mean you're seriously ill | Yes, always, it's the worst sign there is | Yes, you'll never smell again | No, it means you just had a cold
test: Why do we gel our hands coming into class even if they look clean? | Gel kills many germs we can't see | It makes hands smell nice | It makes pencils stick to fingers | It's just a rule with no reason behind it
test: Your friend has a mild cough, no fever and feels fine. UK advice says | He can usually go to school | He must stay home for a whole month | He must go to hospital to be checked | He must wear two masks at home
exam: Identify the agent responsible for the disease designated COVID-19. | The coronavirus designated SARS-CoV-2 | A bacterium transmitted through contaminated water | Exposure to cold weather | A fungus of the respiratory tract
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a pandemic? | A novel disease disseminated across many countries in the absence of immunity | An outbreak confined to a single household | A seasonal increase in colds within one town | A disease affecting animals exclusively
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the development of the COVID-19 vaccines. | They were tested upon tens of thousands of volunteers, building upon decades of research | They were released without any trial whatsoever | They were developed over a period of fifty years | They consist solely of saline solution
exam: It is given that a lateral flow test has returned a negative result. State the correct interpretation. | An early infection may nonetheless be present | Infection is excluded with complete certainty | The person is immune in perpetuity | The test has measured the bodily temperature only
